==English==

===Etymology===
Probably of {{etyl|gmq|en}} origin: compare Danish {{m|da|slumpe||to stumble upon by chance}}, Norwegian {{m|no|slumpe||happen by chance}}, Swedish {{m|sv|slumpa||to sell off}}. Compare also German {{m|de|schlumpen||to trail; draggle; be sloppy}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ʌmp|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[collapse]] [[heavily]] or [[helplessly]].
#: {{ux|en|Exhausted, he '''slumped''' down onto the sofa.}}
#* {{RQ:Chmbrs YngrSt|IX}}
#*: “Heavens!” exclaimed Nina, “the blue-stocking and the fogy!—and yours ''are'' pale blue, Eileen!—you’re about as self-conscious as Drina—'''slumping''' there with your hair tumbling ''à la'' Mérode! Oh, it's very picturesque, of course, but a straight spine and good grooming is better.{{nb...}}”
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[decline]] or [[fall off]] in [[activity]] or [[performance]].
#: {{ux|en|Real estate prices '''slumped''' during the recession.}}
#* {{quote-journal|date=October 29, 2011|author=Phil McNulty|work=BBC Sport
|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/15413593.stm Chelsea 3-5 Arsenal]
|passage=The Gunners captain demonstrated his importance to the team by taking his tally to an outstanding 28 goals in 27 Premier League games as Chelsea '''slumped''' again after their shock defeat at QPR last week.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[slouch]] or [[droop]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[lump]]; to throw together messily.
#* {{w|Sir William Hamilton, 9th Baronet|Sir William Hamilton}} (1788-1856)
#*: These different groups{{...}}are exclusively '''slumped''' together under that sense.
# To fall or sink suddenly through or in, when walking on a surface, as on thawing snow or ice, a bog, etc.
#* {{w|Isaac Barrow}} (1630-1677)
#*: The latter walk on a bottomless quag, into which unawares they may '''slump'''.

